{#
  One segment's worth of the Jonson widget — rendered once per tab (All / General /
  VIPs) by insights.twig, which owns the styles and the tab switching.

  Takes `stats` for its own segment and `presence`, which is deliberately NOT
  segmented: it is a count of who is on the site in the last five minutes, and the
  presence table records no VIP flag. The same figure therefore shows on all three
  tabs, which is honest — it is the same fact — but worth knowing before reading it as
  "VIPs online now".
#}

{# Token counts run to the millions, and "77490" is harder to read at a glance than
   "77k". A MACRO, not a `{% set %}` arrow function: Twig arrow functions only exist to
   be handed to filters like map/filter, and calling one as fmt(...) is a syntax error.

   Defined HERE, in the file that uses it. It lived in insights.twig before this was
   split out, and an include does NOT inherit the parent's imports — so it survived the
   split as a call to a macro that no longer existed anywhere. #}
{% macro tokens(n) %}
    {%- if n >= 1000000 -%}{{ (n / 1000000)|number_format(1) }}m
    {%- elseif n >= 1000 -%}{{ (n / 1000)|round }}k
    {%- else -%}{{ n }}
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}
{% import _self as f %}
{# The health line, and ONLY when there is something wrong. An always-visible row of
       zeros trains you to stop reading it; a row that appears only when it matters gets
       read. apiError means visitors saw the fallback copy instead of an answer. #}
    {% if stats.apiErrors or stats.rateLimited %}
        <div class="jonson-w__warn">
            {% if stats.apiErrors %}{{ stats.apiErrors }} answer{{ stats.apiErrors == 1 ? '' : 's' }} failed — visitors saw the fallback{% endif %}
            {% if stats.apiErrors and stats.rateLimited %} · {% endif %}
            {% if stats.rateLimited %}{{ stats.rateLimited }} question{{ stats.rateLimited == 1 ? '' : 's' }} rate-limited{% endif %}
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="jonson-w__stats">
        {# "total" and "avg" spelled out, so the row reads as counts-then-average rather
           than three numbers of unstated kind. The tooltips carry the period, which the
           labels themselves deliberately don't — see the widget subtitle. #}
        <div class="jonson-w__stat"><div class="jonson-w__n">{{ stats.chats }}</div><div class="jonson-w__label" title="Sessions in which at least one question was asked. A session is one tab, one sitting — it survives navigation and ends when the tab closes.">chat sessions</div></div>
        <div class="jonson-w__stat"><div class="jonson-w__n">{{ stats.questions }}</div><div class="jonson-w__label" title="Questions asked across those sessions">total questions</div></div>
        {# "avg questions", not "avg turns" — same number, but nobody has to ask what a
           turn is. It is the drop-off read: how far a conversation gets before it stops. #}
        <div class="jonson-w__stat"><div class="jonson-w__n">{{ stats.avgTurns }}</div><div class="jonson-w__label" title="Average questions per session — how far a conversation gets before it stops">avg questions</div></div>
        {# How fast Jonson FEELS: the silence before the first word, not how long the
           whole reply takes to finish. A long answer is not a slow one — nobody sits
           there feeling the length of something they are already reading.

           Shown in seconds once it passes a second, because "4.2s" is a wait a person
           recognises and "4200ms" is a number they have to convert. A median, so one
           cold start cannot make a normal week look bad; a dash when nothing in the
           period was measured, rather than a zero that would read as instant. #}
        <div class="jonson-w__stat"><div class="jonson-w__n">{% if stats.waitMs is null %}—{% elseif stats.waitMs >= 1000 %}{{ (stats.waitMs / 1000)|number_format(1) }}s{% else %}{{ stats.waitMs }}ms{% endif %}</div><div class="jonson-w__label" title="Median time from pressing send to the first word appearing. Measures the wait, not the length of the answer — replies that never call the model (the junk gate, API errors) are excluded.">typical wait</div></div>
        {# No "suggestions taken" headline. It was totals over totals — chips offered on
           one turn are pressed on the next, so it could never pair exactly — and the
           Most suggested column below says the same thing properly, per chip, where a
           0% row is actually actionable. One approximate number competing with an exact
           list is worse than the list on its own. #}
        {# "chat → work" to sit alongside "chat → lead": the two are the same shape of
           measure — what share of conversations ended up somewhere that matters — so
           they should read as a pair rather than as two unrelated numbers. #}
        <div class="jonson-w__stat"><div class="jonson-w__n">{{ stats.toWork }}%</div><div class="jonson-w__label" title="Share of conversations that ended by clicking through to a SPECIFIC case study. The /case-studies index counts as browsing, not as work.">chat → work</div></div>
        <div class="jonson-w__stat"><div class="jonson-w__n">{{ stats.toLead }}%</div><div class="jonson-w__label">chat → lead</div></div>
    </div>

    {# CHATS PER DAY — hidden for now, not deleted.

       At current volume it is 29 bars on the 2% floor and one spike, which reads as a
       flat line with a bump rather than as a chart. The thing it is actually good at —
       making an outage or a sudden spike obvious without reading a number — only starts
       working once there is daily traffic to have a shape.

       `stats.daily` is still computed and still returned, so bringing it back is
       deleting this comment and the {% raw %}{% if false %}{% endraw %} around it. #}
    {% if false %}
    {# LABELLED, not just tooltipped. It was neither, and an unlabelled row of bars with
           no axis is a puzzle rather than a chart — the first question anyone asked of it
           was "what is that?". The peak is named too, so the bars have a scale: heights are
           relative to the busiest day, which is meaningless without knowing what that day
           was. #}
        {% if stats.daily|length %}
            {% set peak = max(stats.daily|column('n')) %}
            <div class="jonson-w__h">
                Chats per day
                {% if peak > 0 %}<span style="text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0;">· busiest {{ peak }}</span>{% endif %}
            </div>
            <div class="jonson-w__spark" title="Conversations per day — hover a bar for the date">
                {% for d in stats.daily %}
                    {# A floor of 2% so a quiet day is still a visible tick rather than a gap
                       the eye reads as missing data. #}
                    <i style="height: {{ peak > 0 ? max(2, (d.n / peak * 100)|round) : 2 }}%" title="{{ d.day }}: {{ d.n }}"></i>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        {% endif %}
    {% endif %}

    {# WHO CAME — full width, above the columns, because it is a table rather than a
       ranked list: three fields per row don't fit a third of the widget without the
       names truncating to nothing.

       Only populated on the VIPs tab (see Analytics::vipRows), so on the others this
       block simply isn't rendered rather than sitting there empty.

       Ordered by questions: a door goes to one named person, so "did they engage" beats
       "how often did they turn up". ✓ marks one that produced an enquiry. #}
    {% if stats.vips|length %}
        <div class="jonson-w__h">VIP</div>
        {# A REAL <table>. This was a stack of CSS grids, one per row, each with `auto`
           columns — and independent grids size their columns independently, so the
           header's widths and the body's had no reason to match and didn't. A table
           shares one set of column widths across every row by definition, which is the
           entire problem solved. #}
        <table class="jonson-w__table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Name</th>
                    <th>Last seen</th>
                    <th>Chat sessions</th>
                    <th>Questions</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for vip in stats.vips %}
                    {# `is-alt` from the loop index, NOT nth-child: a VIP owns one summary
                       row plus one row per question, so counting children would stripe
                       the wrong ones. The parent's index keeps a person's rows one
                       shade together. #}
                    <tr class="{{ loop.index is even ? 'is-alt' }}">
                        <td title="{{ vip.name }}">
                            {% if vip.asked|length %}
                                <button type="button" class="jonson-w__vip" data-jonson-vip="{{ vip.id }}" aria-expanded="false">
                                    <span class="jonson-w__caret" aria-hidden="true">▸</span>{{ vip.name }}
                                </button>
                            {% else %}
                                {{ vip.name }}
                            {% endif %}
                            {%- if vip.lead %} <span class="jonson-w__nil" title="Sent an enquiry">✓</span>{% endif %}
                        </td>
                        {# Day/month/year, zero-padded, 24-hour. NOT |datetime('short'), which
                           follows the control panel's locale and renders month-first — the
                           same row would read 09/11 or 11/09 depending on who is logged in. #}
                        <td>{{ vip.last ? vip.last|date('d/m/Y H:i') : '—' }}</td>
                        <td>{{ vip.visits }}</td>
                        <td>{{ vip.questions }}</td>
                    </tr>

                    {# ONE ROW PER QUESTION, sharing the table's own columns rather than a
                       nested list inside a colspan — the question sits under Name and its
                       timestamp under Last seen, so the detail lines up with the header
                       instead of being a block that happens to sit beneath it.

                       Rendered up front and toggled, like the tabs: a handful of lines
                       each, and a panel that opens instantly beats one that fetches. #}
                    {% set alt = loop.index is even %}
                    {% for q in vip.asked %}
                        <tr class="jonson-w__detail {{ alt ? 'is-alt' }}" data-jonson-vip-detail="{{ vip.id }}" hidden>
                            <td>
                                {# Tree connector: ├ while more follow, └ on the last, so a
                                   run of questions reads as belonging to the name above
                                   rather than as loose rows that happen to be indented. #}
                                <span class="jonson-w__branch" aria-hidden="true">{{ loop.last ? '└' : '├' }}</span>
                                {{- ' ' ~ q.question }}
                                {%- if q.fromChip %} <span class="jonson-w__tag" title="They took a suggestion rather than typing it">chip</span>{% endif %}
                                {%- if q.outcome != 'answered' %} <span class="jonson-w__tag" title="This turn was not answered by the model">{{ q.outcome }}</span>{% endif %}
                            </td>
                            <td>{{ q.at ? q.at|date('d/m/Y H:i') : '—' }}</td>
                            <td></td>
                            <td></td>
                        </tr>
                    {% endfor %}
                {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
        </table>
    {% endif %}

    {# Each column below hides itself when its own list is empty, so the panel never
       shows a heading over a dash. But hiding ALL of them would leave a row of zeros
       with nothing under it and no explanation, which reads as broken rather than new —
       hence the single line when there is nothing at all. #}
    {% if not stats.chats %}
        <p class="jonson-w__empty">No data in this period</p>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="jonson-w__cols">

        {% if stats.asked|length %}
        <div class="jonson-w__col--zebra">
            {# The qualifier lives on the tooltip, not in the heading: a column head
               should name the column, and "· typed" was explaining the query rather
               than labelling the list. #}
            <div class="jonson-w__h" title="Questions people typed. Chip clicks are excluded — they record the chip's own wording.">Most asked</div>
            {# Chip clicks are excluded deliberately: they record the CHIP's wording, so
               including them would just reflect Jonson's own suggestions back. #}
            {% for question, n in stats.asked %}
                <div class="jonson-w__row">
                    <span class="jonson-w__count">{{ n }}</span>
                    <span class="jonson-w__txt" title="{{ question }}">{{ question }}</span>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}

        {% if stats.suggested|length %}
        <div class="jonson-w__col--zebra">
            <div class="jonson-w__h" title="Chips Jonson offered, and how many were pressed. Offered ▸ taken.">Most suggested</div>
            {# The 0% rows are why this panel exists: a suggestion shown twenty times and
               never once pressed is one to rewrite. #}
            {% for chip in stats.suggested %}
                <div class="jonson-w__row">
                    <span class="jonson-w__count">{{ chip.offered }}</span>
                    <span class="jonson-w__count {{ chip.taken == 0 ? 'jonson-w__nil' }}">▸{{ chip.taken }}</span>
                    <span class="jonson-w__txt" title="{{ chip.text }} — offered {{ chip.offered }}, taken {{ chip.taken }} ({{ chip.rate }}%)">{{ chip.text }}</span>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}

        {% if stats.origins|length %}
        <div>
            {# PERCENTAGES, to match Ended by. Both columns partition the same thing —
               every conversation starts in exactly one place and ends in exactly one
               way, so both sets sum to 100% of chats — and showing one as counts and
               the other as shares made them look like different kinds of measure.
               The raw count is on the tooltip, which matters at low volume where "50%"
               can mean one conversation out of two.

               Most asked and Most suggested stay as counts on purpose: those are NOT
               partitions. A question can appear in any number of conversations and a
               chip can be offered many times over, so there is no whole for them to be
               a share of. #}
            <div class="jonson-w__h">Started from</div>
            {% set startTotal = stats.chats ?: 1 %}
            {% for path, n in stats.origins %}
                <div class="jonson-w__row">
                    <span class="jonson-w__count">{{ (n / startTotal * 100)|round }}%</span>
                    <span class="jonson-w__txt" title="{{ n }} of {{ stats.chats }} conversations">{{ path }}</span>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}

            <div class="jonson-w__h" style="margin-top: 14px;">Ended by</div>
            {# ?: 1 so a period with no conversations divides by one rather than zero —
               every row is 0% anyway, but Twig throws rather than shrugging. #}
            {% set total = stats.chats ?: 1 %}
            {# One list, commonest first — the ordering and the row-building both live in
               Analytics::endingRows(). It used to render in a fixed funnel order, best
               outcome down to worst, which read nicely but buried whatever actually
               happened.

               Every row is a share of all conversations, so the column sums to 100% —
               the same partition Started from above uses, which is why both are shown
               as percentages rather than counts.

               "closed the tab" also covers "we never saw them go": the beacon can't fire
               on a force-quit. Every other row is therefore a floor, not an exact rate. #}
            {% for row in stats.endingRows %}
                <div class="jonson-w__row">
                    <span class="jonson-w__count">{{ (row.n / total * 100)|round }}%</span>
                    <span class="jonson-w__txt" title="{{ row.label }} — {{ row.n }} of {{ stats.chats }} conversations">{{ row.label }}</span>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
        {% endif %}

    </div>

    <div class="jonson-w__foot">
        {# Tokens per chat uses the window; the month total is a CALENDAR month, because
           that is the period a bill covers. Labelled so the two aren't read as one. #}
        <strong>{{ f.tokens(stats.tokensPerChat) }}</strong> tokens per chat ·
        <strong>{{ f.tokens(stats.tokensMonth) }}</strong> tokens this month
        {% if stats.cacheReadMonth or stats.cacheWriteMonth %}
            · cache {{ f.tokens(stats.cacheReadMonth) }} read / {{ f.tokens(stats.cacheWriteMonth) }} written
        {% endif %}
        · {{ stats.returning }} returning
        {% if stats.junk %}· {{ stats.junk }} junk rejected{% endif %}
    </div>
